## Fertility Was Never Just About Pregnancy

In older folk traditions, fertility was understood as something much wider than conception.

It belonged to the land that sustained people, the animals that carried new life, the trees that flowered each spring, and the women who carried the continuation of their families.

The same symbols appeared again and again.

Seeds represented potential. Water represented renewal. Bread represented nourishment. Fruit trees represented generations. Blood marked the body's cycles and its connection to the earth.

Fertility rituals were woven into everyday life through household customs, seasonal observances, herbal preparations, offerings and prayers.

Some traditions sought blessing. Others focused on protection, restoration or the hope of new beginnings.

This guide explores that world.

It brings together historical folklore, traditional plant symbolism and contemporary ritual practices inspired by older European customs, with particular interest in Balkan and Slavic traditions.

